<Project>

  <!--
    The JSON-RPC packages (polhem-dev/polhem-jsonrpc): the version the framework and its hosts reference, and the
    switch that builds against a local checkout instead.

    Imported by src/Directory.Build.props and by the HTTP hosts (apps/Polhem.Northwind/Polhem.Northwind.Server, tools/Polhem.LoadTests), which reference
    Polhem.JsonRpc.AspNetCore. Like Version.props it is not named Directory.Build.props, so it attaches only where it
    is imported.

    A maintainer changing both repositories at once sets PolhemJsonRpcSource to a local checkout of polhem-jsonrpc in
    PolhemJsonRpc.local.props next to this file, which git ignores, for example:

      <Project><PropertyGroup><PolhemJsonRpcSource>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../polhem-jsonrpc</PolhemJsonRpcSource></PropertyGroup></Project>

    The projects then reference its source instead of the packages. CI and the release workflow never set it, so what
    they build and publish depends on the released packages.

    The version is exact, not a range. Half of the access decision (which methods an action name reaches, the payload
    filter, replay protection) lives in those packages, so a newer one changes the framework's security behavior; a
    range would let that happen in an application without a framework release. Moving to a newer version is a change
    to this line, reviewed like any other. The project templates write the same version, and
    TemplateJsonRpcVersionTests (tests/Polhem.Templates.UnitTests) fails when they disagree.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PolhemJsonRpcVersion>[1.2.0]</PolhemJsonRpcVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)PolhemJsonRpc.local.props"
          Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)PolhemJsonRpc.local.props')" />

</Project>
